'' A BYREF RESULT IS A POINTER. The return TYPE already followed that rule; the result
'' SLOT did not, so 'function = g' over a 'byref as Inner' copied g into a local
'' aggregate and returned the AGGREGATE where the declaration promised a pointer -- it
'' lowered, it verified, and the caller read whichever bytes happened to be there. A
'' byref STRING or ZSTRING result had no slot at all and declined instead.
''
'' Measured: 'struct $5INNER* F( void ) { struct $5INNER* fb$result$1; fb$result$1 =
'' &G$; return fb$result$1; }', and 'char* F1( FBSTRING* S$1 ) { fb$result$1 =
'' *(char**)S$1; }' -- so the slot holds an ADDRESS and 'function = expr' stores the
'' address of expr.
'' ...AND SO IS EVERY 'return' OUT OF ONE, which only the 'byval' spelling used to be.
'' An ordinary 'return p' lowered to the pointee's VALUE and returned it where the
'' declaration promises an ADDRESS: tiko-lang emitted 'func ptr PICK' and then 'ret <the
'' loaded long>'. Measured, fbc's C is 'int32* PICK( ... )' with 'fb$result$1 = P$1'.
''
'' The CALL SITE had the other half: the call took its type from the EXPRESSION, so a
'' pointer came back typed i32 and was used as the number. fbc reads '*vr' at every
'' one. An AGGREGATE referent is left as the pointer -- 'fu( ).a' is
'' '*(int32*)vr$1' to fbc, so field.addr applies to the call's result directly -- and a
'' DISCARDED result is left alone, because there is nothing to read through.
''
'' Nineteen goldens moved when this landed. The note that used to sit at the return
'' path said routing every one through the assignment path was 'a blast radius out of
'' all proportion'; the blast radius was the bug, and all nineteen were the same wrong
'' answer.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/byref-result.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' GOLDEN: expected/byref-result.out
